From ec4692bab5affee077956b8a02bdfce02673dbec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 16:30:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: sign the blob URLs, check ownership, and bound the
 uploads
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cloud saves had no access control worth the name.

1. The 'signed URLs' were not signed. getURL/putURL emitted
   .../<nsa>/<app>/<compId>_<index>.bin?Expires=<unix>&nx=1 and the blob
   handlers never checked Expires, never checked a signature and never required
   a token. The URL WAS the credential, it never expired, and it is printed to
   the log (START_UPLOAD resp=…, COMPONENT_CREATE resp=…) — so a shared log or a
   screenshot handed out permanent authority to re-download or OVERWRITE a
   player's save. They are now HMAC-signed over (operation, owner, title,
   component, index, expiry) and verified before any I/O, so a download link
   cannot be replayed as an upload and an expired link stops working.
   SCSI_ALLOW_UNSIGNED_BLOBS=1 exists only to finish migrating consoles holding
   old URLs, and says so in the log every time it is used.

2. Archive and component operations took only the numeric id: nothing tied the
   id to the caller's account. The ids come from crypto/rand, which makes them
   hard to guess, but that is not an authorisation check — and they travel in
   URLs and logs. A request that carries a VERIFIED identity differing from the
   archive's owner is now refused; a request with no verifiable token keeps the
   previous behaviour, because the console does not always present one and
   tightening that belongs with a token audit.

3. uploadBlob streamed an unbounded body straight to disk, so one client could
   fill the filesystem and take down every player's saves. Now capped
   (SCSI_MAX_BLOB_BYTES, default 64 MiB), and written to a temporary file then
   renamed — a client that vanished mid-upload used to leave a TRUNCATED blob in
   place of the previous complete one, which the player only discovers when they
   restore it. Metadata bodies are capped too.

4. Component fields (status, size, digest) were mutated and read outside the
   store lock, and persist() marshalled the live object while another request
   could be changing it: a data race that could also emit half-updated JSON.
   Mutations and snapshots now happen under the lock, and archive.json — the
   index that maps blobs to their owner — is written atomically.

Tests cover the signature (issued/accepted, unsigned refused, forged refused,
expired refused, bound to one component, download not replayable as upload), the
ownership rule, and the atomic metadata write.
